{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>If he comes in by himself.</b> That is, if he does not yet have a wife but is single — he may not marry a daughter of Israel while he is in servitude, for he has no means to provide her with food, clothing, and conjugal rights, until he completes his term and goes free. Therefore Scripture needed to say 'he shall go out by himself.' Likewise, his master does not give him a Canaanite maidservant, since he entered there without a wife — for it is a commandment of the Torah that the slave leave his master's domain as he entered: if he entered single, he goes out single, and his master does not give him a Canaanite maidservant [and if he entered married, having an Israelite wife, then his master may give him a Canaanite maidservant]. This is what it says: 'if his master gives him a wife.' Or the sense of 'בגפו' may be 'בגופו' — in his body, that is, whole in his body; as he came, so he goes out — he does not go out missing a tooth or an eye or the tips of limbs, like a Canaanite slave." "<b>And know</b> that a Hebrew slave acquires himself by four things, and they are: six years, the Jubilee, the death of the master, and diminution of money. And the bored slave acquires himself by two things: the Jubilee and the death of the master. And the maidservant by five things, for she goes out by signs (of puberty), and she is not bored."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:01.078067771Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}